-A vector space also contains a special zero vector, which we denote by 0 . It satisfies the property that for any other vector $|v\rangle,|v\rangle+0=|v\rangle$. Note that we do not use the ket notation for the zero vector - it is the only exception we shall make. The reason for making the exception is because it is conventional to use the 'obvious' notation for the zero vector, $|0\rangle$, to mean something else entirely. The scalar multiplication operation is such that $z 0=0$ for any complex number $z$. For convenience, we use the notation $\left(z_{1}, \ldots, z_{n}\right)$ to denote a column matrix with entries $z_{1}, \ldots, z_{n}$. In $\mathbf{C}^{n}$ the zero element is $(0,0, \ldots, 0)$. A vector subspace of a vector space $V$ is a subset $W$ of $V$ such that $W$ is also a vector space, that is, $W$ must be closed under scalar multiplication and addition.
